{
  "title": "Build Part B- Enterprise Agent Fleet in Foundry and M365",
  "locked": false,
  "description": "\n\nTo move to production, the local invoice review proof of concept is brought into Foundry, connected to enterprise knowledge sources, with validated behavior inside VS Code, and distributed through M365 to bring agentic services into the everyday flow of Caldova’s team.\n\nProving it locally was step one of the loop. Now we make it enterprise-ready: hosted, grounded in Caldova's data, and available where people already work.\n",
  "rows": [
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~25s",
      "narrative": "We saw how quickly I can build a proof of concept.\n\nMoving it toward production is just as simple - and I never leave the GitHub Copilot app to do it.",
      "demo_actions": "Transition from the local proof of concept to the enterprise build environment. Frame this as the move from local validation into a production-ready deployment path.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "To get my agent - or a whole fleet of them - production-ready, I bring in Microsoft Foundry.\n\nRight from the Copilot app, it connects directly to my Foundry project: authenticate, pick the model, start building.\n\nDeliberately simple.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the relevant code and configuration files in VS Code. Point out the Foundry project connection, authentication, model selection, and the minimal setup required to start building agents.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~15s",
      "narrative": "But the model alone was never the point.\n\nRelying on the model's training would only give me generic answers.\n\nThe power is grounding the agent in Caldova's own knowledge - so it operates like a member of my team, with the context and the in-house IQ we actually have.",
      "demo_actions": "Set up the transition from base model wiring into connected enterprise data and tools.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "That's what Foundry IQ does.\n\nIt lets me ground the agent in our own sources - and it's open: SharePoint, Fabric, Teams, our own systems.\n\nHere, I point it at the one thing this agent needs: our contracts knowledge base.\n\nNow the agent reasons over Caldova's real contracts.\n\nIts recommendations are grounded in the actual terms we agreed to - not the model's best guess.\n\nAnd notice the choice along the way: which model, which knowledge, which surface.\n\nThat's the platform point - this is open, not a single locked destination.",
      "demo_actions": "Show the configured enterprise connections and tool access in VS Code. Call out WorkIQ, FabricIQ, SharePoint, and Teams as knowledge sources the agent can use during reasoning.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~15s",
      "narrative": "And I can test all of it right here, without leaving the app.",
      "demo_actions": "Transition from configuration into testing and inspection inside the editor.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "Same kind of interactive canvas I used locally - now chatting with the grounded agent in Foundry.\n\nWatch the retrieval trace: every answer shows the exact contract clause it pulled.\n\nThat's the difference between a claim and a cited finding.\n\nRe-audit the Aster Ridge invoice - Foundry IQ now catches the $0.50-vs-contract rate and the unauthorized packaging charge, with citations.\n\nYou'll also see the groundwork for evaluation here - hold that thought.\n\nWe come back to it in the evaluation chapter.\n\nRight now, this is still about building.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the Foundry Toolkit panels in VS Code. Show hosted models, playground access, session history, traces, and evaluations, then run a quick interaction and inspect the result.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~20s",
      "narrative": "Once it's behaving the way I want, publishing is one step - from tested agent to hosted deployment.",
      "demo_actions": "Set up the publish step and show the path from tested agent to hosted deployment.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
